Well, I finally ascended a Sporkhack hero!  It took me 45,000 turns or
so and I played pretty cautiously following a saddening defeat last
week.  I'll not bother with a narrative but  just give some general
game comments.

Derek, is there a way to access the dumplog?

I like the new early game poison change as it seems less brutal than
the instadeath in vanilla.  However, it did seem like I lost an
inordinate amount of heros to "extremely toxic" poison in Sporkhack.
But I suppose those would have been insta-deaths in Vanilla so those
went from a harsh RNG's fault to mine own.  In this game I found
poison resistance from some critter and I felt a bit safer.

The dungeon was littered with wands of wishing (3), magic lamps (3)
and magic markers(4 or 5)!    I found a wand of wishing before the
castle, which was a nice help.  My wishes went for:
2 b?oCharging
+3 blessed fixed greased GDSM
+3 blessed fixed helm of brilliance
+3 blessed fixed gauntlets of dexterity
7 blessed wax candles :/  (I lost the ones from Izchak, oops)

I managed to acquire Dirge, the poisoned longsword but by the time I
got it, there were better options available.  Dirge doesn't seem as
analogous to Excalibur as I was expecting.  Excalibur seemed to be
placed such that any early Lawful (Knight in Spork) could have a good
weapon but finding a willing sacrifice for converting my longsword to
Dirge, proved difficult.  As a human, the most obious sacrifice
candidate would be Kops or guards in Minetown, but by the time robbing
shops (and potentially angering the Minetown priest) is a safe idea,
the other artifact weapons are available.  I was fortunate and found a
main dungeon shop with an altar, and oh yeah, a co-aligned unicorn!
With a self-teleport I generated a bunch of Kops and within the same
2000 turns had Dirge, an extra 5 or 10 experience levels, Frostbane,
and was crowned with Stormbringer.  I tried out Dirge for a while but
opted instead for the more common level drain rather than than the
less useful poison.

Once again I enchanted a stack of 40 or so darts and tried to polypile
myself a silver short sword.  I did manage to generate a single silver
dagger after about 15 zaps.

I made it a point to only kill Rodney with melee to test out the
changes to the hit system.  He died 10 times and was eluded with a
potion of paralysis once.  Also, I two-weaponed to see how well Rodney
could do.  He fared much better than in Vanilla and consistently
managed to Double Trouble.  When things got stressful I would switch
to Strombringer alone.  Occasionally he would steal the amulet but
that was usually okay because then I could teleport to the upstairs or
out of the summoning storms.  I never used a wand of death during the
game.

One interesting side effect of monsters recognizing reflection is that
wands tend to have far more charges in them when the hero picks them
up.  I used to bring a cockatrice corpse with me to Orcus because
otherwise he would use up his wand; now he only shoots once or twice
until reflection triggers.  The same goes for the turret sergeants in
the castle.

I like that the Gehennom cavern levels have self-teleport allowed as
it makes them far more navigable.  Also, it makes monsters, covetous
ones in particular far more fun to combat.  One problem with the open
lava pools outside of the cave is that flying monsters can be
generated there or be teleported there and then be trapped.  I was
thinking that if the walls were torn down there would still be a cool
atmosphere and flying monsters could return to burn the hero.  But
maybe that's too Plane-of-fire-ish.  Just a thought.

The other silly thing I did was try to stay mounted during the
Ascension Run.  This was facilitated with the Create Familiar spell.
One thing to note is that Create Familiar for a Dark Knight generates
a pony or a random monster rather than a nightmare or a random
monster.  I went through over a dozen ponies and gave up on them for
the plane of fire since they kept getting killed in one turn.  A black
dragon mount lasted a good while, though.  I just stayed mounted for
fun, it took me longer to drop the amulet and saddle up my pony than
it would have taken me to just keep moving.  I also didn't use jumping
consistently on the ascension run; I couldn't be bothered so I just
walked.

Create Familiar on Astral was fun.  Level 3 might be a little low, but
I suppose time will tell.  I generated an Archon but I didn't see it
do too much.  I had 10 or 12 pets that I would keep near me with a
magic whistle.  Sometimes I used my +6 glaive to bash Death and
priests with from behind the wall of friendlies.  At the end of the
game, I tried to create a few more archons but I became burdened
because I wanted to have my markers and wands in open inventory.
Death started getting too close to me (and withering me) so I figured
that I had better just ascend.  I also considered streaking back
across the Astral Plane for fun but there was a hostile archon and a
hostile titan at the middle round room and they are rarely "fun".

Next time I'll try a wizard (again) but I haven't had much success yet
with that class yet.

